extends layout

block page_intro
  .col-md-12.cardSpacer
    #page-header-container.explorer-hero
      #page-title-container.explorer-hero-brand
        .explorer-hero-copy
          h3#page-title How To Mine Defcoin
          .sub-page-header.text-muted
            | Beginner-friendly wallet setup, real hardware examples, and live mining estimates grounded in the current DC903 pool and Defcoin network conditions.
        +defcoinPageBranding('explorer-hero-mark')
    .cardSpacer.clearfix

block content
  script(type='application/json', id='defcoinMiningCatalogData') !{JSON.stringify(miningCatalog)}
  script(src=assetPath('/js/defcoin-mine.js'))
  .col-12
    .card.card-default.border-0.cardSpacer
      .card-header
        strong 1. Install A Wallet First
      .card-body
        .defcoin-callout
          p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
            | The currently published binaries come from the official
            a.ms-1(href=miningCatalog.releases.githubReleaseUrl)= miningCatalog.releases.version
            |  release and are all x86_64-era builds. Windows and Linux downloads are current enough to test today. Intel macOS builds exist, but no Apple Silicon build is currently published or verified.
          p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
            | If you use Apple Silicon, assume you need either Rosetta experiments or a future native community build. Do not treat Apple Silicon support as working until someone verifies it.
        .defcoin-callout.mt-3
          p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
            | You do not need the full desktop wallet before you can receive coins. You can generate a paper wallet first, save the private key offline, share only the public address, and ask someone to send Defcoin there. Later you can import or sweep that paper wallet into a full wallet.
        .table-responsive
          table.table.table-bordered.table-striped.table-hover
            thead
              tr
                th Platform
                th Asset
                th Notes
            tbody
              each wallet in miningCatalog.releases.downloads
                tr
                  td= wallet.id
                  td
                    a(href=wallet.url)= wallet.label
                  td= wallet.compatibility

  .col-12
    .card.card-default.border-0.cardSpacer
      .card-header
        strong 2. Ways To Get Defcoin
      .card-body
        p.defcoin-helper
          | Mining is only one path. If you are just trying to get your first working wallet, it is often easier to receive a small transfer first, then test mining second.
        ul
          each method in miningCatalog.obtainMethods
            li= method
        p.defcoin-helper.mt-3.mb-0
          | If you want the lightest possible first step, use the
          a.ms-1(href='https://defcoin.dc903.org/paperwallet') Paper Wallet
          |  page to create an address pair now, keep the private key secret, and share the public address with a friend or counterparty.

  .col-12
    .card.card-default.border-0.cardSpacer
      .card-header
        strong 3. Pick A Mining Path That Matches Your Gear
      .card-body
        .defcoin-page-grid.two-up
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label CPU Mining
            p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
              | Best when you already own the machine and want to learn the basics. It is simple and quiet, but usually the slowest path.
            p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
              | Good fit: a desktop CPU you already have running, such as the Ryzen 9 3900X in the table below.
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label GPU Mining
            p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
              | Better than CPU mining if you already own an older gaming card. Setup is a little more involved, but it is still approachable.
            p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
              | Good fit: a used RX 580-class card, especially if you want to experiment before buying an ASIC.
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label ASIC Mining
            p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
              | This is the serious Scrypt-mining route. It brings the most hashrate, but it also brings fan noise, heat, and power draw.
            p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
              | Good fit: an older L3+ or a smaller home miner like the Mini-DOGE III if you want better odds without building a rack.
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label Reality Check
            p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
              | Defcoin is a low-activity network in 2026. The numbers below are estimates, not promises, and small changes in who is mining can swing the results a lot.
            p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
              | Start small, watch your power cost, and treat early mining as learning and collecting rather than guaranteed profit.

  .col-12
    .card.card-default.border-0.cardSpacer
      .card-header
        strong 4. Example Hardware And Live Estimates
      .card-body
        .defcoin-callout
          p.mb-2.defcoin-helper
            | The price ranges below are curated 2026 used-market snapshots, not scraped live every second. The mining estimates do refresh from current site data so the yield columns track the live pool context instead of a frozen article.
          p.mb-0.defcoin-helper
            | Two yield views are shown: a recent-pool-pace view and a long-run expectation view. On a tiny, low-activity network they may be close, but both are still estimates, not guarantees.
        .defcoin-status-grid
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label Catalog Checked
            span.stat-value#miningCatalogChecked= miningCatalog.checkedOn
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label Default Power Cost
            span.stat-value#miningPowerCostDefault $0.12 / kWh
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label Current Pool Context
            span.stat-value#miningPoolContext Loading live estimates
          .defcoin-stat-card
            span.stat-label Current Network Context
            span.stat-value#miningNetworkContext Loading live estimates
        .table-responsive
          table#mining-estimates-table.table.table-bordered.table-striped.table-hover
            thead
              tr
                th Rig
                th Type
                th Hashrate
                th Watts
                th Used Price Range
                th Power Cost / Day
                th Recent Pool Pace
                th Long-Run Expected
                th Sources
            tbody
              tr
                td.text-center(colspan='9') Loading live mining estimates

  .col-12
    .card.card-default.border-0.cardSpacer
      .card-header
        strong 5. Basic Beginner Flow
      .card-body
        ol
          li Install a wallet first and make sure you can open it, receive to a fresh address, and back up the wallet or private key.
          li Obtain a small amount of Defcoin by gift, transfer, paper wallet, or mining so you can test that your wallet and explorer lookups work.
          li Decide whether you are learning with a CPU or GPU you already own, or buying an ASIC on purpose.
          li Compare your power cost against the live estimates below before you point hardware at the pool.
          li Start mining to an address you control, then watch the Pool page and Explorer so you understand how shares, balances, and found blocks actually look in practice.
